LOK: a font name sets Western text, and CJK text when it can

Writer applies a chosen font name to the scripts of the selection, or
with no selection to the input language's script. Under LOK the input
language is the worker host's keyboard, not the user's: on Linux a
font picked at the cursor set only the Western font and Chinese typed
after kept the old one (GitHub ElyOffice #7), while on a host with a
Chinese keyboard a Western font replaced the Chinese one. LOK now
follows Word: the name always sets the Western font, and also the CJK
font when the font is installed and has CJK glyphs. The font state at
a bare cursor likewise follows the script there, not the keyboard.
getCommandValues(".uno:CharFonts") reads the Western and Asian font
at the cursor or over the selection, empty when mixed, so a client
can confirm the font it set where the state shows another script;
past Writer's attribute lookup limit it says "unread", not defaults.

